## What it says
`TauCeti.LieTypeIndex.InStandardRange` is a condition on Lie-type indices, `LieTypeIndex → Prop`: the conventional rank and small-field ranges of the families of finite groups of Lie type. Its value is defined by cases, with $q = p^e$ the field parameter:

- $A_n(q)$: $n \geq 1$, and $q \geq 4$ when $n = 1$, which drops $A_1(2) \cong S_3$ and $A_1(3) \cong A_4$;
- ${}^2A_n(q)$: $n \geq 2$, and $q \geq 3$ when $n = 2$, which drops the solvable $\mathrm{PSU}_3(2)$, of order $72$;
- $B_n(q)$: $n \geq 2$, except $B_2(2) \cong S_6$;
- $C_n(q)$: $n \geq 3$ and $p \neq 2$;
- $D_n(q)$ and ${}^2D_n(q)$: $n \geq 4$;
- $G_2(q)$: $q \geq 3$, which drops $G_2(2)$, not simple;
- the Suzuki and Ree families: $m \geq 1$, which drops ${}^2B_2(2)$, ${}^2G_2(3)$ and ${}^2F_4(2)$, none of them simple;
- $E_6$, $E_7$, $E_8$, $F_4$, ${}^2E_6$, ${}^3D_4$ and the Tits group: no condition (`True`).

These are the ranges of the standard list of finite simple groups of Lie type. Each excluded index is either not simple or isomorphic to a group listed elsewhere: $B_2(q) \cong C_2(q)$ always, and $B_n(q) \cong C_n(q)$ in characteristic two, both kept under $B$, as the docstring says; $D_3(q) \cong A_3(q)$ and ${}^2D_3(q) \cong {}^2A_3(q)$; the derived groups of $B_2(2)$, $G_2(2)$, ${}^2G_2(3)$ and ${}^2F_4(2)$ are $A_6$, ${}^2A_2(3)$, $A_1(8)$ and the Tits group. So the ranges drop no simple group.

## References and tests
- ATLAS of Finite Groups — the rank and small-field ranges of the families of groups of Lie type.
- Gorenstein–Lyons–Solomon — the same ranges.

No test is recorded.

## Lean subtleties
- `q.card` is the number $p^e$ that the prime-power parameter stands for, and `q.p` is its prime $p$, so `q.p ≠ 2` is odd characteristic.
- Ranks and $m$ are natural numbers, starting at $0$; the lower bounds rule out the small values.
- It is indexing data, not a statement about groups: nothing here asserts that any group is finite or simple.
- Isolated coincidences, such as $A_1(4) \cong A_1(5)$, lie within these ranges: the condition removes only the non-simple members and the systematic overlaps the docstring names.
